menu "Discrete inputs (Blue Raven staging / recovery)"

config DISCRETE_IN_MIN_INTERVAL_MS
    int "Minimum interval between accepted edges (ms, 0 = accept every edge)"
    range 0 5000
    default 50
    help
        An edge arriving sooner than this after the last ACCEPTED one is
        counted as suppressed and otherwise ignored. The first edge is
        always accepted, with its exact ISR-captured timestamp -- this
        only ever discards what FOLLOWS a real signal.

        This is not cosmetic. These tasks run at the highest priority in
        the system (FC_PRIO_DISCRETE, above the sensor readers), and
        each accepted edge writes a console line and pushes an event to
        the card and the downlink. An input that chatters therefore
        starves everything below it: a floating GPIO23 on the bench
        produced ~6700 edges/s and timed out the I2C sensor reads, which
        looked like a bus fault and was not one.

        A Blue Raven cannot assert the same line twice within 50 ms, and
        staging and recovery are separate pins with separate counters,
        so the default cannot discard a real deployment signal. Lower it
        only if you have a specific reason to expect faster genuine
        pulses on ONE line.

        0 restores the old accept-everything behaviour.

choice DISCRETE_IN_MODE
    prompt "How a Blue Raven line is read"
    default DISCRETE_IN_MODE_ANALOG
    help
        ANALOG (default): the ADC reads each line's voltage every
        DISCRETE_IN_SAMPLE_MS and compares it with this page's thresholds.
        Needed on this vehicle: the Blue Raven runs from 1S, and with a 2k
        series resistor its lines measure ~2.86 V idle and 3.7 V fired
        (2026-10-10). Both are above the digital HIGH level (fixed in the
        silicon), so a digital input would never see the change.

        DIGITAL: the original GPIO rising-edge interrupt. Exact ISR
        timestamps, but only works when idle is well below ~0.8 V and
        fired well above ~2.5 V at the pin.

config DISCRETE_IN_MODE_ANALOG
    bool "Analog: ADC with voltage thresholds"

config DISCRETE_IN_MODE_DIGITAL
    bool "Digital: GPIO rising-edge interrupt"

endchoice

config DISCRETE_IN_DIVIDER_TOP_OHMS
    int "Series resistor, line to pin (ohms)"
    depends on DISCRETE_IN_MODE_ANALOG
    range 0 1000000
    default 2000
    help
        R6 (staging) / R9 (recovery). Both lines use the same values.
        Only used to convert the pin voltage back to the line voltage when
        there IS a resistor to ground below; with none, the pin sees the
        line voltage directly (no current flows into the ADC input).

config DISCRETE_IN_DIVIDER_BOTTOM_OHMS
    int "Resistor, pin to ground (ohms; 0 = none fitted)"
    depends on DISCRETE_IN_MODE_ANALOG
    range 0 1000000
    default 0
    help
        R5 (staging) / R8 (recovery). 0 means no resistor to ground: the
        pin reads the line voltage 1:1, which is the 2k-series setup of
        2026-10-10.

        The ADC tops out around 3.1 V at the pin. A FIRE threshold must be
        below that at the pin or it can never be seen -- the boot log says
        so in red. A fired level ABOVE full scale is fine: it reads as
        full scale, which is still above the threshold. What must NOT clip
        is the idle level, or idle and fired look the same.

        CHECK THESE AGAINST A METER: the boot log prints each line's
        reading at start ("line reads N mV"). With the resistors set
        right it matches what a meter on the line shows.

config DISCRETE_IN_STAGING_FIRE_MV
    int "Staging: FIRED at or above (mV on the Blue Raven line)"
    depends on DISCRETE_IN_MODE_ANALOG
    range 100 30000
    default 3000
    help
        On the line, where a meter reads it. Default 3.0 V: above the
        ~2.86 V idle, and 0.7 V below the 3.7 V fired level, so a 1S
        Blue Raven battery sagging while it drives an e-match is still
        seen down to 3.0 V.

        The margin that is SMALL is the idle side (~0.14 V). If the idle
        level creeps up -- a different e-match, a different resistor --
        check the boot log's reading against this.

config DISCRETE_IN_STAGING_RELEASE_MV
    int "Staging: idle again at or below (mV on the Blue Raven line)"
    depends on DISCRETE_IN_MODE_ANALOG
    range 0 30000
    default 2950
    help
        Below the fire threshold, so noise on a line sitting near one
        threshold cannot make an edge per wobble (hysteresis). Must stay
        above the idle level (~2.86 V) or the line can never return to
        idle after a firing. Clamped to the fire threshold if set above.

config DISCRETE_IN_RECOVERY_FIRE_MV
    int "Recovery: FIRED at or above (mV on the Blue Raven line)"
    depends on DISCRETE_IN_MODE_ANALOG
    range 100 30000
    default 3000

config DISCRETE_IN_RECOVERY_RELEASE_MV
    int "Recovery: idle again at or below (mV on the Blue Raven line)"
    depends on DISCRETE_IN_MODE_ANALOG
    range 0 30000
    default 2950

config DISCRETE_IN_SAMPLE_MS
    int "Sample period (ms)"
    depends on DISCRETE_IN_MODE_ANALOG
    range 1 50
    default 2
    help
        How often each line is read. Sets the timestamp resolution of a
        detected firing, together with the confirm count below.

config DISCRETE_IN_CONFIRM_SAMPLES
    int "Consecutive samples needed to change state"
    depends on DISCRETE_IN_MODE_ANALOG
    range 1 50
    default 3
    help
        A firing is believed after this many samples in a row at or above
        the fire threshold (6 ms at the defaults). The recorded time is
        that of the FIRST of them.

config DISCRETE_IN_INTERNAL_PULLDOWN
    bool "Enable the internal pulldown on discrete inputs"
    depends on DISCRETE_IN_MODE_DIGITAL
    default n
    help
        OFF for flight, and for any build running on PCB v1.

        The board already holds each input low through its divider
        (R5/R8 on staging, R8/R9 on recovery). Enabling the internal
        pulldown (~45k) in parallel with those shifts the divider ratio
        from 0.434 to 0.415, which raises the voltage the Blue Raven
        must produce before the pin reads HIGH -- it makes a real
        trigger HARDER to see, and the window in PCB_v1_wiring.md
        assumes 0.434.

        Turn it ON only when the divider is not present -- a bare module
        on a breadboard, for instance -- where the pin would otherwise
        float and generate thousands of spurious edges a second.

endmenu
